WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.000
Gravity

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.000
EMCS centrifuge: zero, lunar, Mars and Earth gravity

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:15.000
Endosperm rupture: 24.1 h at zero gravity, 26.8 h at Earth gravity. The gap comes from how water wets the blotter, an assumed input.

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:26.000
Once the root is out, it turns toward gravity, except at zero gravity, where it keeps growing straight.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:40.000
At 48 h the root has turned 59° at lunar gravity, 75° at Mars gravity and 86° at Earth gravity.

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:58.000
Every level above zero ends with a vertical root (90° at lunar gravity): partial gravity changes how fast the root turns, not where it ends up.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:15.000
The gravity response comes from a separate model of auxin redistribution in the root tip (virtual-root): a model output, not a measurement.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.000
Sealed and dark: BRIC-PDFU in orbit and on the ground

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:31.000
The same sealed dish, in orbit and at Earth gravity. No light, no air exchange.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:45.000
Rupture at 29.1 h in orbit and 32.3 h on the ground: again set by the assumed wetting of the substrate.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:02:00.000
Without gravity or light the seedling has no up or down: the root and hypocotyl keep growing the way the embryo pointed in the seed.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:10.000
On the ground the root has turned 90°; in orbit, 0°.
